module gunbc.rust_item_scan

import extdeps.languages.rust.syntax { rust_item_forms }
import extdeps.rust.std_thread_local { thread_local_declared_item_keyword, thread_local_macro_name }
import std.syntax { ExprBody }
import std.decimal { decimal_digit_char }
import gunbc.rust_item_identity {
  RustConst,
  RustEnum,
  RustFunction,
  RustImplBlock,
  RustImplMethod,
  RustItemIdentity,
  RustItemKind,
  RustModule,
  RustStatic,
  RustStruct,
  RustTrait,
  RustTypeAlias,
  rust_item_identity_key
}
import std.algebra { trim, list_snoc_item, freemonoid_empty }
import std.types { Bool, Int, List, NonEmptyStr, String }
import v2.std.algebra { any, filter, fold_list, length, list_map }

// WHY THIS READER EXISTS, AND WHAT CAPABILITY IT RESTORES.
//
// The item-grain census that gunbc.seed_growth_admission's completeness join needs had exactly one
// realization -- scripts/rust_item_census.py -- and it was deleted by gunbc#9132 with the rest of
// the measurement corpus. Nothing replaced it, so from 2026-08-24 there was NO ROUTE in this
// repository to enumerate Rust items at identity grain. THAT DROP WAS NEVER DECLARED, and it could
// not have been caught by the delete-first census DESIGN section 3 relies on: the census's
// consumers were lanes rather than modules, so no dependent could refuse when it went. It is also
// a live instance of DESIGN section 4b(3)'s own rule that a restoration trigger must name the
// CAPABILITY -- rust_item_host_observation_scaffold_note's dissolution read "producer lands and
// enrolls; Python script deleted", a conjunction whose second half an unrelated cut satisfied while
// the producer never landed.
//
// THE DELETED SCRIPT'S DEFECT IS NOT REPRODUCED HERE, and that is the reason this is authored in
// .dag rather than transliterated. Its ITEM_PATTERNS were a SECOND roster of Rust item keywords,
// authored beside -- and independent of -- the grammar authority that already owns them. That is
// the DESIGN section 3 nicknaming violation, and the census predicted its own failure from it:
// gunbc.bare_reference_scanner_admission records the script over-reporting one diff by 100 percent,
// counting nine fixture declarations authored inside Rust raw string literals as Rust items. Here
// the item-keyword population is READ FROM extdeps.languages.rust.syntax rust_item_forms. There is
// no second roster; a keyword the grammar authority does not carry is not an item here, by
// construction rather than by diligence.
//
// WHAT THIS IS NOT: a Rust parser. No ingest grammar for Rust exists in this tree -- the rust
// authority carries item FORMS and lexical spellings for emission, not a parse table -- so this
// reader recognizes item HEADERS and their extents by scope depth over rustfmt-normalized source,
// and REFUSES on any structural line it does not recognize. That refusal is what makes an
// incomplete recognizer safe: incompleteness surfaces as a typed, located line-stop rather than as
// a smaller population, which is the empty-observation narrow DESIGN section 5 forbids. It is also
// what keeps the raw-string defect above from recurring in a new spelling: source inside a raw
// string literal that reaches a scope boundary is not a recognized structural line, so it refuses
// instead of minting a phantom item.
// Item-grain reader over rustfmt-normalized Rust source. Item keywords are read from
// extdeps.languages.rust.syntax rust_item_forms and are not re-coined; every structural line the
// recognizer cannot classify is a typed, located refusal rather than a dropped line. Replaces the
// deleted scripts/rust_item_census.py, whose regex ITEM_PATTERNS were the second roster this reader
// refuses to author. THREE CONSTRUCTS ARE RECOGNIZED AS EXTENTS THAT DECLARE NOTHING -- a wrapped
// use list, a wrapped attribute, and a macro invocation block -- because rustfmt continues each
// past its first line and a reader that met the continuation at a scope boundary would refuse most
// of the population it exists to census. A macro block additionally records its REGION: the grammar
// authority carries no macro item form, so a declaration made by a macro cannot be named here, and
// a changed line inside one is reported UNATTRIBUTED rather than file-level, because a declaration
// added inside a macro is hand-Rust growth and file-level is the answer that would admit it
// silently. ONE MACRO IS THE EXCEPTION, BY UPSTREAM CITATION RATHER THAN BY THIS READER'S CHOICE:
// extdeps.rust.std_thread_local records that std's thread_local! body is a sequence of `static`
// declarations, so its block is a scope whose statics are named items of the enclosing module.

// THE RECOGNIZER'S PRECONDITION IS A REPOSITORY FACT, NOT AN ASSUMPTION. Extents are decided by
// scope depth: a scope opened at indent 4*d contains structural lines at indent 4*(d+1), and closes
// at a line that is exactly a closing brace at its own indent. That is rustfmt's normal form, and
// `cargo fmt --all --check` is a documented check over this tree, so the precondition is one the
// repository already holds rather than one this reader hopes for. WHERE IT DOES NOT HOLD THE READER
// REFUSES: a file whose bodies are not so normalized reaches a line it cannot classify and returns
// RustFileScanRefused naming the line and its text. It never guesses an extent, because a guessed
// extent silently mis-attributes a changed line to the wrong item -- the one failure mode that
// would let the admission join report green over an unenumerated addition.

// INLINE MODULES ARE SCOPES, NOT LEAVES, because the citation vocabulary already treats them that
// way. gunbc.declaration_index_seed_growth cites seven declarations under
// v1_compiler.cli_run.bare_reference_scanner_tests -- an inline `mod` inside cli_run.rs whose
// members are cited individually. A reader that recorded the mod as one opaque item would make
// those seven citations unjoinable, so the scan descends and each item carries the dotted path of
// its enclosing inline modules. An impl block inside a module nests the same way; a method's
// impl_subject comes from the nearest enclosing impl, never from a module.

// FOUR SCOPES THAT RECORD NOTHING SIT BESIDE THE THREE THAT DO, and each exists because rustfmt
// wraps a construct across lines that this reader would otherwise meet at a scope boundary and
// refuse. A `use` list, a derive attribute and a macro invocation all continue past their first
// line; none of them is an item, so none records one. Measured on the seed's hand-maintained Rust
// before they existed, wrapped `use` lists alone refused 58 files -- a recognizer safe enough to be
// useless, which is the failure mode a fail-closed reader has to be watched for.
// THE RAW LITERAL AN ITEM HAS OPEN, WITH WHERE IT OPENED, OR NONE. One variant rather than a
// terminator string beside a flag, so "opened on the header with no terminator" has no constructor.
type RawLiteral
  = NoRawLiteral
  | OpenRawLiteral { terminator: String, opened_on_header: Bool }

fn raw_literal_of(terminator: String, opened_on_header: Bool) -> RawLiteral {
  if terminator == "" { NoRawLiteral } else { OpenRawLiteral { terminator: terminator, opened_on_header: opened_on_header } }
}

type RustScanScope
  = ModuleScope { module_name: String, start_line: Int }
  | UseScope { start_line: Int }
  | AttributeScope { start_line: Int }
  | MacroScope { macro_name: String, start_line: Int }
  | ThreadLocalScope { start_line: Int }
  | ImplScope { impl_subject: String, start_line: Int }
  | ItemScope {
      kind: RustItemKind,
      item_name: String,
      impl_subject: String,
      enclosing_module: String,
      start_line: Int,
      raw_literal: RawLiteral,
      initializer_continued: Bool
    }

// A MACRO INVOCATION'S BLOCK IS AN EXTENT WITH NO ITEM, AND A CHANGE INSIDE IT IS UNATTRIBUTED.
//
// `macro_rules! name { … }` and `lazy_static! { … }` declare real things, and this reader cannot
// name them: the grammar authority carries no macro item form, and inventing one here would be the
// second roster this module exists not to author. Recognizing the EXTENT keeps the file scannable.
// Reporting a change inside it as file-level would be the fail-open: a declaration added inside a
// macro is hand-Rust growth. So the region is recorded, a changed line inside it is UNATTRIBUTED,
// and the admission join stops the line. Named ceiling, not silence.
//
// THREAD_LOCAL! IS NOT A MACRO REGION, because its grammar is an upstream fact rather than a guess:
// std documents its body as "any number of static declarations", each with optional attributes and
// visibility (extdeps.rust.std_thread_local, cited at the pinned 1.93.0 docs). ThreadLocalScope
// admits exactly those lines -- a `static` header, an attribute, a comment -- and refuses anything
// else, so a static added inside it is an ADDED item by name, and a body the citation does not
// describe still stops the line. Every other macro stays at the ceiling above.
type RustMacroRegion {
  macro_name: String
  start_line: Int
  loc: Int
}

type RustLineAttribution
  = AttributedToItem { item_key: String }
  | AttributedToFileLevel
  | AttributedToUnnamedMacroBlock { macro_name: String, line: Int }
  | AttributionOutOfRange { line: Int, line_count: Int }

type RustFileScan
  = RustFileScanned {
      repo_path: String,
      items: List<RustItemIdentity>,
      macro_regions: List<RustMacroRegion>,
      line_count: Int
    }
  | RustFileScanRefused {
      repo_path: String,
      line: Int,
      line_text: String,
      detail: String
    }

type RustScanState {
  repo_path: String
  line_number: Int
  items: List<RustItemIdentity>
  macro_regions: List<RustMacroRegion>
  scopes: List<RustScanScope>
  attach_start: Int
  refusal_line: Int
  refusal_text: String
  refusal_detail: String
  continues_string: Bool
}

fn rust_item_keywords() -> List<String> {
  list_map(xs: rust_item_forms, f: fn(form) { form.keyword })
}

fn rust_keyword_is_item_form(word: String) -> Bool {
  any(xs: rust_item_keywords(), predicate: fn(k) { k == word })
}

// TOTAL OVER THE AUTHORITY'S KEYWORDS AND OVER NOTHING ELSE. std.syntax ItemFormKind collapses
// trait, impl, const and static into OtherForm, so the kind cannot be read off the form's kind
// field alone; the keyword decides. Membership still comes from the authority -- every caller has
// already asked rust_keyword_is_item_form -- so this never widens the population, it only names
// what the authority admitted. rust_item_kind_keyword_mapping_is_total is the executing evidence
// that the two stay in step: a keyword added to the grammar authority and not named here reds it,
// rather than silently acquiring a plausible kind.
fn rust_item_kind_for_keyword(word: String) -> RustItemKind {
  if word == "fn" { RustFunction }
  else if word == "struct" { RustStruct }
  else if word == "enum" { RustEnum }
  else if word == "type" { RustTypeAlias }
  else if word == "trait" { RustTrait }
  else if word == "impl" { RustImplBlock }
  else if word == "mod" { RustModule }
  else if word == "static" { RustStatic }
  else { RustConst }
}

fn rust_item_kind_mapping_named_keywords() -> List<String> {
  ["fn", "struct", "enum", "type", "trait", "impl", "mod", "static", "const"]
}

fn rust_item_kind_keyword_mapping_is_total() -> Bool {
  fold_list(
    xs: rust_item_keywords(),
    empty: true,
    cons: fn(ok, k) {
      ok && any(xs: rust_item_kind_mapping_named_keywords(), predicate: fn(n) { n == k })
    }
  )
}

fn char_is_ident(c: String) -> Bool {
  (c >= "a" && c <= "z")
    || (c >= "A" && c <= "Z")
    || decimal_digit_char(ch: c)
    || c == "_"
}

fn leading_ident_from(s: String, at: Int, acc: String) -> String {
  if at >= string_length(s: s) {
    acc
  } else {
    let c = char_at(s: s, pos: at)
    if char_is_ident(c: c) {
      leading_ident_from(s: s, at: at + 1, acc: concat(acc, c))
    } else {
      acc
    }
  }
}

fn leading_ident(s: String) -> String {
  leading_ident_from(s: s, at: 0, acc: "")
}

fn drop_prefix_length(s: String, n: Int) -> String {
  if n >= string_length(s: s) { "" } else { substring(s: s, start: n, end: string_length(s: s)) }
}

fn indent_width_from(s: String, at: Int) -> Int {
  if at >= string_length(s: s) {
    at
  } else if char_at(s: s, pos: at) == " " {
    indent_width_from(s: s, at: at + 1)
  } else {
    at
  }
}

fn indent_width(s: String) -> Int {
  indent_width_from(s: s, at: 0)
}

fn scan_past_char_from(s: String, at: Int, target: String) -> Int {
  if at >= string_length(s: s) {
    at
  } else if char_at(s: s, pos: at) == target {
    at + 1
  } else {
    scan_past_char_from(s: s, at: at + 1, target: target)
  }
}

data rust_item_modifier_words: List<String> = ["pub", "async", "unsafe", "extern", "default"]

fn word_is_item_modifier(word: String) -> Bool {
  any(xs: rust_item_modifier_words, predicate: fn(m) { m == word })
}

// `const` IS THE ONE SPELLING THAT IS BOTH A MODIFIER AND AN ITEM KEYWORD. It opens a constant on
// its own and modifies a function when `fn` follows, so the peel consults the NEXT word rather than
// the current one. Getting this wrong in either direction is a silent mis-classification -- `const
// fn foo` recorded as a constant named `fn`, or `const C` dropped entirely -- so the discriminating
// pair is authored as a witness rather than argued for here.
fn peel_item_modifiers(s: String, fuel: Int) -> String {
  if fuel <= 0 {
    s
  } else {
    let t = trim(s: s)
    if starts_with(s: t, prefix: "pub(") {
      peel_item_modifiers(
        s: drop_prefix_length(s: t, n: scan_past_char_from(s: t, at: 0, target: ")")),
        fuel: fuel - 1
      )
    } else if starts_with(s: t, prefix: "\"") {
      peel_item_modifiers(
        s: drop_prefix_length(s: t, n: scan_past_char_from(s: t, at: 1, target: "\"")),
        fuel: fuel - 1
      )
    } else {
      let w = leading_ident(s: t)
      if w == "const" {
        let rest = trim(s: drop_prefix_length(s: t, n: string_length(s: w)))
        if leading_ident(s: rest) == "fn" { peel_item_modifiers(s: rest, fuel: fuel - 1) } else { t }
      } else if word_is_item_modifier(word: w) {
        peel_item_modifiers(s: drop_prefix_length(s: t, n: string_length(s: w)), fuel: fuel - 1)
      } else {
        t
      }
    }
  }
}

fn peeled_item_head(text: String) -> String {
  peel_item_modifiers(s: text, fuel: 8)
}

// THE IMPL SUBJECT IS THE TEXT THE CITATION VOCABULARY ALREADY USES. The uncitable item this
// repository has carried in prose since the Cargo-manifest bridge is spelled
// "std::fmt::Display for Stage0CargoBinManifestParseRefusal", so the subject recovered here is the
// header text after `impl` and its generics with the body brace and any where-clause removed --
// exactly that string. Recovering some other normal form would fork one identity into two
// spellings that coincide only until someone edits either.
fn strip_impl_generics(s: String) -> String {
  let t = trim(s: s)
  if starts_with(s: t, prefix: "<") {
    trim(s: drop_prefix_length(s: t, n: scan_past_char_from(s: t, at: 0, target: ">")))
  } else {
    t
  }
}

fn trim_trailing_marker(s: String, marker: String) -> String {
  let t = trim(s: s)
  let n = string_length(s: t)
  let m = string_length(s: marker)
  if n >= m && substring(s: t, start: n - m, end: n) == marker {
    trim(s: substring(s: t, start: 0, end: n - m))
  } else {
    t
  }
}

fn impl_subject_from_head(rest: String) -> String {
  let without_generics = strip_impl_generics(s: rest)
  let without_brace = trim_trailing_marker(
    s: trim_trailing_marker(s: without_generics, marker: "{"),
    marker: ";"
  )
  trim_trailing_marker(s: without_brace, marker: "where")
}

fn line_is_blank(text: String) -> Bool {
  trim(s: text) == ""
}

// EXPORTED BECAUSE A SECOND READER ASKS IT: gunbc.rust_item_host_observation's reference producer
// skips exactly the lines this scan skips, so "is this line a comment or attribute" has one
// answer in one place. It is a LEADING test: a trailing `// ...` after code, or a `/* */` block,
// is not recognised here, and neither reader claims otherwise.
fn line_is_attribute_or_comment(text: String) -> Bool {
  let t = trim(s: text)
  starts_with(s: t, prefix: "#[")
    || starts_with(s: t, prefix: "#![")
    || starts_with(s: t, prefix: "//")
}

fn line_is_file_level_declaration(text: String) -> Bool {
  let w = leading_ident(s: peeled_item_head(text: text))
  w == "use" || w == "crate"
}

// A TRAILING `//` COMMENT IS NOT PART OF THE DECLARATION IT FOLLOWS. `const LEN: usize = 8 + 16; //
// offset, length` ends at its semicolon; reading the comment's last character as the line's end
// left the const open, and it swallowed the const after it (resolved_graph_cache.rs
// PART_DESCRIPTOR_LEN over V3_HEADER_LEN) -- silently. A `//` opens a comment when it is outside a
// string literal, and on one line that is decided by the parity of the quotes before it. A `//`
// with an odd count before it is inside a literal (a URL), and the search continues past it.
fn code_before_line_comment_from(text: String, at: Int) -> String {
  let found = find_from(s: text, at: at, pattern: "//")
  if found < 0 {
    text
  } else if quote_count_from(s: substring(s: text, start: 0, end: found), at: 0, acc: 0) % 2 == 0 {
    substring(s: text, start: 0, end: found)
  } else {
    code_before_line_comment_from(text: text, at: found + 2)
  }
}

fn quote_count_from(s: String, at: Int, acc: Int) -> Int {
  if at >= string_length(s: s) {
    acc
  } else if char_at(s: s, pos: at) == "\"" {
    quote_count_from(s: s, at: at + 1, acc: acc + 1)
  } else {
    quote_count_from(s: s, at: at + 1, acc: acc)
  }
}

fn code_before_line_comment(text: String) -> String {
  code_before_line_comment_from(text: text, at: 0)
}

// A HEADER CLOSES ON ITS OWN LINE when it carries no block that outlives it: a `;`-terminated
// declaration, or a whole item written on one line. Otherwise it opens a scope that closes at a
// brace at its own indent. There is no third case, and a line matching neither at a scope boundary
// is exactly the state this reader refuses on.
fn head_closes_on_its_own_line(text: String) -> Bool {
  let t = trim(s: code_before_line_comment(text: text))
  let n = string_length(s: t)
  if n == 0 {
    true
  } else {
    let last = substring(s: t, start: n - 1, end: n)
    last == ";" || (last == "}" && string_contains(s: t, pattern: "{"))
  }
}

fn hash_run_from(s: String, at: Int, acc: Int) -> Int {
  if at >= string_length(s: s) {
    acc
  } else if char_at(s: s, pos: at) == "#" {
    hash_run_from(s: s, at: at + 1, acc: acc + 1)
  } else {
    acc
  }
}

fn repeat_hashes(n: Int, acc: String) -> String {
  if n <= 0 { acc } else { repeat_hashes(n: n - 1, acc: concat(acc, "#")) }
}

// A RAW STRING IS THE ONE CONSTRUCT THAT PUTS ARBITRARY TEXT AT A SCOPE BOUNDARY, and it is the
// exact construct the deleted census misread: gunbc.bare_reference_scanner_admission records it
// counting nine .dag declarations authored inside Rust raw string literals as Rust items. The
// terminator is recovered from the opening delimiter's own hash count -- which is what makes the
// literal self-describing and the recovery decidable rather than a guess -- and while it is open
// every line is skipped, so no text inside a literal can be read as structure.
//
// A LITERAL OPENED IN A BODY IS THE SAME LEXICAL FACT AS ONE OPENED ON A HEADER, so it is tracked
// the same way. An item's extent ends at its own closing brace, and a brace inside a string literal
// is not a closing brace -- that is what an item IS, read lexically, and it holds wherever the
// literal opens. Tracking only header-opened literals made the reader treat a body literal's own
// column-zero `}` as the item's close: it closed the enclosing fn early and then refused the
// literal's tail at the scope boundary, which put every witness binary that authors a .dag fixture
// in a `let src = r#"...` out of reach (gunbc#12886's census stopped there, on
// interp_recorded_fixture_witness.rs). Worse than the refusal, a literal whose text happened to
// read as Rust items after the premature close would have minted phantom items -- the deleted
// script's defect arriving by a second route. So every item line is read for an UNCLOSED raw
// literal: the opener must begin a token (an `r` or `br` not continuing an identifier, which is
// what keeps `"error"` from reading as `r"`), its terminator is recovered from its own hash count,
// and a literal closed on the same line is skipped so a second opener after it is still seen.
//
// WHAT THIS STILL DOES NOT COVER, named rather than left to be found: an ordinary (non-raw) string
// literal continued across lines, and a raw-string opener spelled inside an ordinary string or a
// trailing comment. Neither has a self-describing terminator this reader can recover without a
// lexer, so neither is tracked; if one carries a column-zero brace the premature close leaves text
// at a scope boundary and the file REFUSES. A whole-line `//` comment is skipped, since its text
// is never code. The next rung is a modeled Rust lexer, at which point this whole recognizer
// deletes in its favour.
fn find_from(s: String, at: Int, pattern: String) -> Int {
  let n = string_length(s: s)
  let m = string_length(s: pattern)
  if at + m > n {
    0 - 1
  } else if substring(s: s, start: at, end: at + m) == pattern {
    at
  } else {
    find_from(s: s, at: at + 1, pattern: pattern)
  }
}

fn raw_string_opener_begins_token(s: String, at: Int) -> Bool {
  if at == 0 {
    true
  } else if !char_is_ident(c: char_at(s: s, pos: at - 1)) {
    true
  } else {
    char_at(s: s, pos: at - 1) == "b" && (at == 1 || !char_is_ident(c: char_at(s: s, pos: at - 2)))
  }
}

fn raw_string_unclosed_from(s: String, at: Int) -> String {
  if at >= string_length(s: s) {
    ""
  } else if char_at(s: s, pos: at) != "r" || !raw_string_opener_begins_token(s: s, at: at) {
    raw_string_unclosed_from(s: s, at: at + 1)
  } else {
    let hashes = hash_run_from(s: s, at: at + 1, acc: 0)
    if at + 1 + hashes < string_length(s: s)
      && char_at(s: s, pos: at + 1 + hashes) == "\"" {
      let terminator = concat("\"", repeat_hashes(n: hashes, acc: ""))
      let close = find_from(s: s, at: at + 2 + hashes, pattern: terminator)
      if close < 0 {
        terminator
      } else {
        raw_string_unclosed_from(s: s, at: close + string_length(s: terminator))
      }
    } else {
      raw_string_unclosed_from(s: s, at: at + 1)
    }
  }
}

// The terminator of the raw string literal this line leaves OPEN, or "" when it leaves none.
fn raw_string_terminator(text: String) -> String {
  if starts_with(s: trim(s: text), prefix: "//") { "" } else { raw_string_unclosed_from(s: text, at: 0) }
}

// The terminator still open after the line that closes `raw`: a literal closed on this line may be
// followed by another opened on it.
fn raw_string_terminator_after_close(text: String, raw: String) -> String {
  let close = find_from(s: text, at: 0, pattern: raw)
  if close < 0 { raw } else { raw_string_unclosed_from(s: text, at: close + string_length(s: raw)) }
}

fn scan_top_raw_terminator(scopes: List<RustScanScope>) -> String {
  fold_list(
    xs: list_last_scope(xs: scopes),
    empty: "",
    cons: fn(_acc, scope) {
      match scope {
        ItemScope {
          kind: _,
          item_name: _,
          impl_subject: _,
          enclosing_module: _,
          start_line: _,
          raw_literal: literal,
          initializer_continued: _
        } =>
          match literal {
            NoRawLiteral => ""
            OpenRawLiteral { terminator: t, opened_on_header: _ } => t
          }
        ModuleScope { module_name: _, start_line: _ } => ""
        ImplScope { impl_subject: _, start_line: _ } => ""
        UseScope { start_line: _ } => ""
        AttributeScope { start_line: _ } => ""
        MacroScope { macro_name: _, start_line: _ } => ""
        ThreadLocalScope { start_line: _ } => ""
      }
    }
  )
}

// A LITERAL OPENED ON THE HEADER IS THE ITEM'S OWN VALUE, so the line that ends it may end the item
// (`pub const SRC: &str = r#"` ... `"#;`). A literal opened in a body is an expression inside the
// item, so the line that ends it is body: its `"#;` ends a statement, never the item, and reading it
// as the close would end a fn at its first fixture.
fn scan_top_raw_opened_on_header(scopes: List<RustScanScope>) -> Bool {
  fold_list(
    xs: list_last_scope(xs: scopes),
    empty: false,
    cons: fn(_acc, scope) {
      match scope {
        ItemScope {
          kind: _,
          item_name: _,
          impl_subject: _,
          enclosing_module: _,
          start_line: _,
          raw_literal: literal,
          initializer_continued: _
        } =>
          match literal {
            NoRawLiteral => false
            OpenRawLiteral { terminator: _, opened_on_header: on_header } => on_header
          }
        ModuleScope { module_name: _, start_line: _ } => false
        ImplScope { impl_subject: _, start_line: _ } => false
        UseScope { start_line: _ } => false
        AttributeScope { start_line: _ } => false
        MacroScope { macro_name: _, start_line: _ } => false
        ThreadLocalScope { start_line: _ } => false
      }
    }
  )
}

// AN EXPRESSION-BODIED ITEM WHOSE HEADER ENDS AT `=` ENDS AT ITS OWN SEMICOLON, ONE INDENT DEEPER.
// rustfmt breaks `static NAME: T =` before a long initializer and continues it at the next indent,
// so the declaration's closing `;` is on a line at that continuation indent, not at the item's own.
// Reading only the item's own indent left such an item open past its semicolon: a top-level
// `static PROVIDER_BOOTSTRAP_STORE_SKIPS: AtomicUsize =` in cli_run.rs swallowed the fn after it
// until that fn's `}`, so the fn was never an item and an edit to it was attributed to the static --
// a silent mis-attribution, not a refusal. The continuation indent is the item's scope boundary, so
// the test is the boundary line that ends the statement; a deeper line is the initializer's body.
fn scan_top_initializer_continued(scopes: List<RustScanScope>) -> Bool {
  fold_list(
    xs: list_last_scope(xs: scopes),
    empty: false,
    cons: fn(_acc, scope) {
      match scope {
        ItemScope {
          kind: _,
          item_name: _,
          impl_subject: _,
          enclosing_module: _,
          start_line: _,
          raw_literal: _,
          initializer_continued: continued
        } => continued
        ModuleScope { module_name: _, start_line: _ } => false
        ImplScope { impl_subject: _, start_line: _ } => false
        UseScope { start_line: _ } => false
        AttributeScope { start_line: _ } => false
        MacroScope { macro_name: _, start_line: _ } => false
        ThreadLocalScope { start_line: _ } => false
      }
    }
  )
}

fn scan_line_closes_top_item(state: RustScanState, text: String) -> Bool {
  let indent = indent_width(s: text)
  let expected = scan_expected_indent(state: state)
  if scan_top_initializer_continued(scopes: state.scopes) {
    indent == expected && ends_with(s: trim(s: text), suffix: ";")
  } else {
    indent == expected - 4 && line_closes_item(text: text)
  }
}

fn scan_state_with_raw(state: RustScanState, raw: String) -> RustScanState {
  fold_list(
    xs: list_last_scope(xs: state.scopes),
    empty: state,
    cons: fn(_acc, scope) {
      match scope {
        ItemScope {
          kind: kind,
          item_name: name,
          impl_subject: subject,
          enclosing_module: enclosing,
          start_line: start,
          raw_literal: _,
          initializer_continued: continued
        } =>
          scan_state_of(
            state: state,
            items: state.items,
            scopes: list_snoc_item(
              xs: list_drop_last(xs: state.scopes),
              item: ItemScope {
                kind: kind,
                item_name: name,
                impl_subject: subject,
                enclosing_module: enclosing,
                start_line: start,
                raw_literal: raw_literal_of(terminator: raw, opened_on_header: false),
                initializer_continued: continued
              }
            ),
            attach_start: state.attach_start
          )
        ModuleScope { module_name: _, start_line: _ } => state
        ImplScope { impl_subject: _, start_line: _ } => state
        UseScope { start_line: _ } => state
        AttributeScope { start_line: _ } => state
        MacroScope { macro_name: _, start_line: _ } => state
        ThreadLocalScope { start_line: _ } => state
      }
    }
  )
}

// AN ITEM CLOSES AT ITS OWN INDENT ON A LINE THAT ENDS THE DECLARATION, and the test admits exactly
// two shapes because rustfmt produces exactly two. A block item ends on a line beginning with its
// closing brace -- `}` for a struct or a fn, `});` for a static built from a closure, `];` for a
// slice literal, all of which begin with the brace or bracket that opened them; a declaration
// without a surviving block ends on a line terminated by a semicolon. What the test deliberately
// does NOT admit is any other line at the item's indent, which is why a wrapped signature's `) ->
// T {` is body rather than a close, and why arbitrary text is far more likely to refuse than to
// close an item early.
fn line_closes_item(text: String) -> Bool {
  let t = trim(s: text)
  starts_with(s: t, prefix: "}") || starts_with(s: t, prefix: "]") || ends_with(s: t, suffix: ";")
}

fn scope_start_line(scope: RustScanScope) -> Int {
  match scope {
    ModuleScope { module_name: _, start_line: n } => n
    UseScope { start_line: n } => n
    AttributeScope { start_line: n } => n
    MacroScope { macro_name: _, start_line: n } => n
    ThreadLocalScope { start_line: n } => n
    ImplScope { impl_subject: _, start_line: n } => n
    ItemScope {
      kind: _,
      item_name: _,
      impl_subject: _,
      enclosing_module: _,
      start_line: n,
      raw_literal: _,
      initializer_continued: _
    } => n
  }
}

fn list_drop_last<T>(xs: List<T>) -> List<T> {
  let n = length(xs: xs)
  fold_list(
    xs: xs,
    empty: freemonoid_empty(),
    cons: fn(acc, x) { if length(xs: acc) + 1 >= n { acc } else { list_snoc_item(xs: acc, item: x) } }
  )
}

fn list_last_scope(xs: List<RustScanScope>) -> List<RustScanScope> {
  fold_list(xs: xs, empty: freemonoid_empty(), cons: fn(_acc, x) { [x] })
}

fn scan_scope_depth(state: RustScanState) -> Int {
  length(xs: state.scopes)
}

fn scan_expected_indent(state: RustScanState) -> Int {
  4 * scan_scope_depth(state: state)
}

fn scan_enclosing_module_path(scopes: List<RustScanScope>) -> String {
  fold_list(
    xs: scopes,
    empty: "",
    cons: fn(acc, scope) {
      match scope {
        ModuleScope { module_name: name, start_line: _ } =>
          if acc == "" { name } else { concat(acc, concat(".", name)) }
        ImplScope { impl_subject: _, start_line: _ } => acc
        UseScope { start_line: _ } => acc
        AttributeScope { start_line: _ } => acc
        MacroScope { macro_name: _, start_line: _ } => acc
        ThreadLocalScope { start_line: _ } => acc
        ItemScope {
          kind: _,
          item_name: _,
          impl_subject: _,
          enclosing_module: _,
          start_line: _,
          raw_literal: _,
          initializer_continued: _
        } => acc
      }
    }
  )
}

fn scan_enclosing_impl_subject(scopes: List<RustScanScope>) -> String {
  fold_list(
    xs: scopes,
    empty: "",
    cons: fn(acc, scope) {
      match scope {
        ImplScope { impl_subject: subject, start_line: _ } => subject
        ModuleScope { module_name: _, start_line: _ } => acc
        UseScope { start_line: _ } => acc
        AttributeScope { start_line: _ } => acc
        MacroScope { macro_name: _, start_line: _ } => acc
        ThreadLocalScope { start_line: _ } => acc
        ItemScope {
          kind: _,
          item_name: _,
          impl_subject: _,
          enclosing_module: _,
          start_line: _,
          raw_literal: _,
          initializer_continued: _
        } => acc
      }
    }
  )
}

fn scan_state_refused(state: RustScanState) -> Bool {
  state.refusal_line > 0
}

fn scan_state_of(
  state: RustScanState,
  items: List<RustItemIdentity>,
  scopes: List<RustScanScope>,
  attach_start: Int
) -> RustScanState {
  RustScanState {
    repo_path: state.repo_path,
    line_number: state.line_number,
    items: items,
    macro_regions: state.macro_regions,
    scopes: scopes,
    attach_start: attach_start,
    refusal_line: 0,
    refusal_text: "",
    refusal_detail: "",
    continues_string: state.continues_string
  }
}

fn scan_state_refuse(state: RustScanState, text: String, detail: String) -> RustScanState {
  RustScanState {
    repo_path: state.repo_path,
    line_number: state.line_number,
    items: state.items,
    macro_regions: state.macro_regions,
    scopes: state.scopes,
    attach_start: state.attach_start,
    refusal_line: state.line_number,
    refusal_text: text,
    refusal_detail: detail,
    continues_string: state.continues_string
  }
}

fn scan_state_advance(state: RustScanState) -> RustScanState {
  RustScanState {
    repo_path: state.repo_path,
    line_number: state.line_number + 1,
    items: state.items,
    macro_regions: state.macro_regions,
    scopes: state.scopes,
    attach_start: state.attach_start,
    refusal_line: state.refusal_line,
    refusal_text: state.refusal_text,
    refusal_detail: state.refusal_detail,
    continues_string: state.continues_string
  }
}

fn scan_item_start_line(state: RustScanState) -> Int {
  if state.attach_start > 0 { state.attach_start } else { state.line_number }
}

fn scan_item_of_scope(
  state: RustScanState,
  scope: RustScanScope,
  end_line: Int
) -> List<RustItemIdentity> {
  match scope {
    UseScope { start_line: _ } => state.items
    AttributeScope { start_line: _ } => state.items
    MacroScope { macro_name: _, start_line: _ } => state.items
    ThreadLocalScope { start_line: _ } => state.items
    ModuleScope { module_name: name, start_line: start } =>
      list_snoc_item(
        xs: state.items,
        item: RustItemIdentity {
          repo_path: state.repo_path,
          kind: RustModule,
          name: name as NonEmptyStr,
          impl_subject: "",
          enclosing_module: scan_enclosing_module_path(scopes: list_drop_last(xs: state.scopes)),
          start_line: start,
          loc: end_line - start + 1
        }
      )
    ImplScope { impl_subject: subject, start_line: start } =>
      list_snoc_item(
        xs: state.items,
        item: RustItemIdentity {
          repo_path: state.repo_path,
          kind: RustImplBlock,
          name: subject as NonEmptyStr,
          impl_subject: "",
          enclosing_module: scan_enclosing_module_path(scopes: list_drop_last(xs: state.scopes)),
          start_line: start,
          loc: end_line - start + 1
        }
      )
    ItemScope {
      kind: kind,
      item_name: name,
      impl_subject: subject,
      enclosing_module: enclosing,
      start_line: start,
      raw_literal: _,
      initializer_continued: _
    } =>
      list_snoc_item(
        xs: state.items,
        item: RustItemIdentity {
          repo_path: state.repo_path,
          kind: kind,
          name: name as NonEmptyStr,
          impl_subject: subject,
          enclosing_module: enclosing,
          start_line: start,
          loc: end_line - start + 1
        }
      )
  }
}

fn scan_state_with_macro_region(state: RustScanState, scope: RustScanScope) -> RustScanState {
  match scope {
    MacroScope { macro_name: name, start_line: start } =>
      RustScanState {
        repo_path: state.repo_path,
        line_number: state.line_number,
        items: state.items,
        macro_regions: list_snoc_item(
          xs: state.macro_regions,
          item: RustMacroRegion {
            macro_name: name,
            start_line: start,
            loc: state.line_number - start + 1
          }
        ),
        scopes: state.scopes,
        attach_start: state.attach_start,
        refusal_line: state.refusal_line,
        refusal_text: state.refusal_text,
        refusal_detail: state.refusal_detail,
        continues_string: state.continues_string
      }
    ThreadLocalScope { start_line: _ } => state
    UseScope { start_line: _ } => state
    AttributeScope { start_line: _ } => state
    ModuleScope { module_name: _, start_line: _ } => state
    ImplScope { impl_subject: _, start_line: _ } => state
    ItemScope {
      kind: _,
      item_name: _,
      impl_subject: _,
      enclosing_module: _,
      start_line: _,
      raw_literal: _,
      initializer_continued: _
    } => state
  }
}

fn scan_close_scope(state: RustScanState) -> RustScanState {
  fold_list(
    xs: list_last_scope(xs: state.scopes),
    empty: scan_state_refuse(
      state: state,
      text: "}",
      detail: "closing brace with no open scope"
    ),
    cons: fn(_acc, scope) {
      scan_state_of(
        state: scan_state_with_macro_region(state: state, scope: scope),
        items: scan_item_of_scope(state: state, scope: scope, end_line: state.line_number),
        scopes: list_drop_last(xs: state.scopes),
        attach_start: 0
      )
    }
  )
}

fn scan_open_header(state: RustScanState, text: String) -> RustScanState {
  let head = peeled_item_head(text: text)
  let keyword = leading_ident(s: head)
  let rest = trim(s: drop_prefix_length(s: head, n: string_length(s: keyword)))
  let start = scan_item_start_line(state: state)
  let enclosing = scan_enclosing_module_path(scopes: state.scopes)
  let self_closing = head_closes_on_its_own_line(text: text)
  if keyword == "impl" {
    let subject = impl_subject_from_head(rest: rest)
    if subject == "" {
      scan_state_refuse(state: state, text: text, detail: "impl header carries no subject")
    } else {
      let scope = ImplScope { impl_subject: subject, start_line: start }
      if self_closing {
        scan_state_of(
          state: state,
          items: scan_item_of_scope(state: state, scope: scope, end_line: state.line_number),
          scopes: state.scopes,
          attach_start: 0
        )
      } else {
        scan_state_of(
          state: state,
          items: state.items,
          scopes: list_snoc_item(xs: state.scopes, item: scope),
          attach_start: 0
        )
      }
    }
  } else {
    let name = leading_ident(s: rest)
    if name == "" {
      scan_state_refuse(
        state: state,
        text: text,
        detail: concat(keyword, " item header carries no name")
      )
    } else if keyword == "mod" && !self_closing {
      scan_state_of(
        state: state,
        items: state.items,
        scopes: list_snoc_item(
          xs: state.scopes,
          item: ModuleScope { module_name: name, start_line: start }
        ),
        attach_start: 0
      )
    } else {
      let impl_subject = scan_enclosing_impl_subject(scopes: state.scopes)
      let kind =
        if keyword == "fn" && impl_subject != "" { RustImplMethod }
        else { rust_item_kind_for_keyword(word: keyword) }
      let header_raw = if self_closing { "" } else { raw_string_terminator(text: text) }
      let scope = ItemScope {
        kind: kind,
        item_name: name,
        impl_subject: if kind == RustImplMethod { impl_subject } else { "" },
        enclosing_module: enclosing,
        start_line: start,
        raw_literal: raw_literal_of(terminator: header_raw, opened_on_header: true),
        initializer_continued: !self_closing && ends_with(s: trim(s: text), suffix: "=")
      }
      if self_closing {
        scan_state_of(
          state: state,
          items: scan_item_of_scope(state: state, scope: scope, end_line: state.line_number),
          scopes: state.scopes,
          attach_start: 0
        )
      } else {
        scan_state_of(
          state: state,
          items: state.items,
          scopes: list_snoc_item(xs: state.scopes, item: scope),
          attach_start: 0
        )
      }
    }
  }
}

fn scan_scope_is_item(scopes: List<RustScanScope>) -> Bool {
  fold_list(
    xs: list_last_scope(xs: scopes),
    empty: false,
    cons: fn(_acc, scope) {
      match scope {
        ItemScope {
          kind: _,
          item_name: _,
          impl_subject: _,
          enclosing_module: _,
          start_line: _,
          raw_literal: _,
          initializer_continued: _
        } => true
        ModuleScope { module_name: _, start_line: _ } => false
        ImplScope { impl_subject: _, start_line: _ } => false
        UseScope { start_line: _ } => false
        AttributeScope { start_line: _ } => false
        MacroScope { macro_name: _, start_line: _ } => false
        ThreadLocalScope { start_line: _ } => false
      }
    }
  )
}

// THE STEP ASKS ONE QUESTION AND REFUSES WHEN IT CANNOT ANSWER IT: is this line at the current
// scope boundary. Deeper lines are body and are never inspected -- which is why this reader needs
// neither brace matching nor string-literal tracking, since it never asks a question whose answer
// depends on them. A line SHALLOWER than the boundary, or one at the boundary that is not a
// recognized structural form, is the state that refuses; that is the arm the deleted script did not
// have, and its absence is why the script silently minted phantom items from raw-string fixtures.
// CLOSING A WRAPPED ATTRIBUTE KEEPS ITS ATTACHMENT, which the ordinary scope close would drop. An
// attribute belongs to the declaration BELOW it, so the item's extent must begin at the attribute
// rather than at its own header line; the general close resets that attachment because every other
// scope genuinely ends a region. Losing it here would not change any item's identity, but it would
// leave the attribute's own lines outside every item, so an edit to a derive would be reported as
// file-level -- an under-attribution, which is the quiet direction.
fn scan_close_attribute_scope(state: RustScanState) -> RustScanState {
  fold_list(
    xs: list_last_scope(xs: state.scopes),
    empty: state,
    cons: fn(_acc, scope) {
      scan_state_of(
        state: state,
        items: state.items,
        scopes: list_drop_last(xs: state.scopes),
        attach_start: scope_start_line(scope: scope)
      )
    }
  )
}

fn scan_top_scope_kind(scopes: List<RustScanScope>) -> String {
  fold_list(
    xs: list_last_scope(xs: scopes),
    empty: "",
    cons: fn(_acc, scope) {
      match scope {
        UseScope { start_line: _ } => "use"
        AttributeScope { start_line: _ } => "attribute"
        MacroScope { macro_name: _, start_line: _ } => "macro"
        ThreadLocalScope { start_line: _ } => "thread_local"
        ModuleScope { module_name: _, start_line: _ } => "module"
        ImplScope { impl_subject: _, start_line: _ } => "impl"
        ItemScope {
          kind: _,
          item_name: _,
          impl_subject: _,
          enclosing_module: _,
          start_line: _,
          raw_literal: _,
          initializer_continued: _
        } => "item"
      }
    }
  )
}

fn line_opens_macro_invocation(text: String) -> Bool {
  let t = trim(s: text)
  let name = leading_ident(s: t)
  name != ""
    && starts_with(s: trim(s: drop_prefix_length(s: t, n: string_length(s: name))), prefix: "!")
}

// A LINE ENDING IN A BACKSLASH CONTINUES A STRING LITERAL, SO THE NEXT LINE IS LITERAL TEXT. In Rust
// source a backslash-newline is an escape that exists only inside a string literal, so the line after
// it is that literal's text at whatever indent its author chose -- column zero, or five -- and never
// structure. rustfmt does not re-indent it, which is why indent alone misreads it: a continued line
// beginning with `}` at an item's own indent read as that item's close, and the line that ends the
// literal with `";` sat at no indent the close tests asked about. Inside a raw literal a backslash is
// an ordinary character, and a comment's trailing backslash continues nothing.
fn scan_state_mark_continuation(state: RustScanState, text: String) -> RustScanState {
  let continues =
    scan_top_raw_terminator(scopes: state.scopes) == ""
      && !starts_with(s: trim(s: text), prefix: "//")
      && ends_with(s: text, suffix: "\\")
  RustScanState {
    repo_path: state.repo_path,
    line_number: state.line_number,
    items: state.items,
    macro_regions: state.macro_regions,
    scopes: state.scopes,
    attach_start: state.attach_start,
    refusal_line: state.refusal_line,
    refusal_text: state.refusal_text,
    refusal_detail: state.refusal_detail,
    continues_string: continues
  }
}

// THE EXPRESSION-BODIED FORMS ARE THE GRAMMAR AUTHORITY'S, NOT A LIST KEPT HERE: an item of a kind
// whose rust_item_forms row carries ExprBody (`const`, `static`) is a declaration ended by its own
// semicolon, so the line that finishes a continued string literal with `;` ends it. A fn body ends at
// its brace and is never ended that way, which is what keeps a `let s = "...\` inside a body from
// closing the fn.
fn scan_top_item_is_expression_bodied(scopes: List<RustScanScope>) -> Bool {
  fold_list(
    xs: list_last_scope(xs: scopes),
    empty: false,
    cons: fn(_acc, scope) {
      match scope {
        ItemScope {
          kind: kind,
          item_name: _,
          impl_subject: _,
          enclosing_module: _,
          start_line: _,
          raw_literal: _,
          initializer_continued: _
        } =>
          any(
            xs: rust_item_forms,
            predicate: fn(form) { form.body_kind == ExprBody && rust_item_kind_for_keyword(word: form.keyword) == kind }
          )
        ModuleScope { module_name: _, start_line: _ } => false
        ImplScope { impl_subject: _, start_line: _ } => false
        UseScope { start_line: _ } => false
        AttributeScope { start_line: _ } => false
        MacroScope { macro_name: _, start_line: _ } => false
        ThreadLocalScope { start_line: _ } => false
      }
    }
  )
}

fn scan_step(state: RustScanState, text: String) -> RustScanState {
  if scan_state_refused(state: state) {
    scan_state_advance(state: state)
  } else if line_is_blank(text: text) {
    scan_state_advance(state: scan_state_of(state: state, items: state.items, scopes: state.scopes, attach_start: 0))
  } else if scan_top_scope_kind(scopes: state.scopes) == "use" {
    if ends_with(s: trim(s: text), suffix: ";") {
      scan_state_advance(state: scan_close_scope(state: state))
    } else {
      scan_state_advance(state: state)
    }
  } else if scan_top_scope_kind(scopes: state.scopes) == "attribute" {
    if ends_with(s: trim(s: text), suffix: "]") {
      scan_state_advance(state: scan_close_attribute_scope(state: state))
    } else {
      scan_state_advance(state: state)
    }
  } else if scan_top_scope_kind(scopes: state.scopes) == "macro" {
    if indent_width(s: text) == scan_expected_indent(state: state) - 4
      && line_closes_item(text: text) {
      scan_state_advance(state: scan_close_scope(state: state))
    } else {
      scan_state_advance(state: state)
    }
  } else {
    let indent = indent_width(s: text)
    let expected = scan_expected_indent(state: state)
    let inside_item = scan_scope_is_item(scopes: state.scopes)
    if inside_item {
      let raw = scan_top_raw_terminator(scopes: state.scopes)
      if raw != "" {
        if !string_contains(s: text, pattern: raw) {
          scan_state_advance(state: state)
        } else if scan_top_raw_opened_on_header(scopes: state.scopes)
          && indent == expected - 4
          && line_closes_item(text: text) {
          scan_state_advance(state: scan_close_scope(state: state))
        } else if scan_top_initializer_continued(scopes: state.scopes)
          && raw_string_terminator_after_close(text: text, raw: raw) == ""
          && ends_with(s: trim(s: text), suffix: ";") {
          scan_state_advance(state: scan_close_scope(state: state))
        } else {
          scan_state_advance(
            state: scan_state_with_raw(state: state, raw: raw_string_terminator_after_close(text: text, raw: raw))
          )
        }
      } else if state.continues_string {
        if scan_top_item_is_expression_bodied(scopes: state.scopes)
          && !ends_with(s: text, suffix: "\\")
          && ends_with(s: trim(s: text), suffix: ";") {
          scan_state_advance(state: scan_close_scope(state: state))
        } else {
          scan_state_advance(state: state)
        }
      } else if scan_line_closes_top_item(state: state, text: text) {
        scan_state_advance(state: scan_close_scope(state: state))
      } else if indent == expected - 4
        && rust_keyword_is_item_form(word: leading_ident(s: peeled_item_head(text: text))) {
        scan_state_advance(
          state: scan_state_refuse(
            state: state,
            text: text,
            detail: "an item header at the open item's own indent: the open item never closed, and reading on would swallow this item into its extent"
          )
        )
      } else {
        let opened = raw_string_terminator(text: text)
        if opened == "" {
          scan_state_advance(state: state)
        } else {
          scan_state_advance(state: scan_state_with_raw(state: state, raw: opened))
        }
      }
    } else if indent > expected {
      scan_state_advance(
        state: scan_state_refuse(
          state: state,
          text: text,
          detail: "line is indented past the current scope boundary but no item is open at it"
        )
      )
    } else if indent < expected {
      if trim(s: text) == "}" && indent == expected - 4 {
        scan_state_advance(state: scan_close_scope(state: state))
      } else {
        scan_state_advance(
          state: scan_state_refuse(
            state: state,
            text: text,
            detail: "line is shallower than the current scope boundary and does not close it"
          )
        )
      }
    } else if scan_top_scope_kind(scopes: state.scopes) == "thread_local"
      && !line_is_attribute_or_comment(text: text)
      && leading_ident(s: peeled_item_head(text: text)) != thread_local_declared_item_keyword {
      scan_state_advance(
        state: scan_state_refuse(
          state: state,
          text: text,
          detail: "a thread_local! body line is neither a static declaration, an attribute nor a comment"
        )
      )
    } else if line_is_attribute_or_comment(text: text) {
      let attaching = scan_state_of(
        state: state,
        items: state.items,
        scopes: state.scopes,
        attach_start: if state.attach_start > 0 { state.attach_start } else { state.line_number }
      )
      if starts_with(s: trim(s: text), prefix: "//") || ends_with(s: trim(s: text), suffix: "]") {
        scan_state_advance(state: attaching)
      } else {
        scan_state_advance(
          state: scan_state_of(
            state: attaching,
            items: attaching.items,
            scopes: list_snoc_item(
              xs: attaching.scopes,
              item: AttributeScope { start_line: state.line_number }
            ),
            attach_start: attaching.attach_start
          )
        )
      }
    } else if line_is_file_level_declaration(text: text) {
      if ends_with(s: trim(s: text), suffix: ";") {
        scan_state_advance(state: scan_state_of(state: state, items: state.items, scopes: state.scopes, attach_start: 0))
      } else {
        scan_state_advance(
          state: scan_state_of(
            state: state,
            items: state.items,
            scopes: list_snoc_item(xs: state.scopes, item: UseScope { start_line: state.line_number }),
            attach_start: 0
          )
        )
      }
    } else if line_opens_macro_invocation(text: text)
      && leading_ident(s: trim(s: text)) == thread_local_macro_name {
      if head_closes_on_its_own_line(text: text) {
        scan_state_advance(
          state: scan_state_refuse(
            state: state,
            text: text,
            detail: "a one-line thread_local! invocation is not split into its static declarations"
          )
        )
      } else if !rust_keyword_is_item_form(word: thread_local_declared_item_keyword) {
        scan_state_advance(
          state: scan_state_refuse(
            state: state,
            text: text,
            detail: "thread_local!'s declared item keyword is not an item form the rust grammar authority names"
          )
        )
      } else {
        scan_state_advance(
          state: scan_state_of(
            state: state,
            items: state.items,
            scopes: list_snoc_item(
              xs: state.scopes,
              item: ThreadLocalScope {
                start_line: if state.attach_start > 0 { state.attach_start } else { state.line_number }
              }
            ),
            attach_start: 0
          )
        )
      }
    } else if line_opens_macro_invocation(text: text) {
      if head_closes_on_its_own_line(text: text) {
        scan_state_advance(state: scan_state_of(state: state, items: state.items, scopes: state.scopes, attach_start: 0))
      } else {
        scan_state_advance(
          state: scan_state_of(
            state: state,
            items: state.items,
            scopes: list_snoc_item(
              xs: state.scopes,
              item: MacroScope {
                macro_name: leading_ident(s: trim(s: text)),
                start_line: if state.attach_start > 0 { state.attach_start } else { state.line_number }
              }
            ),
            attach_start: 0
          )
        )
      }
    } else if rust_keyword_is_item_form(word: leading_ident(s: peeled_item_head(text: text))) {
      scan_state_advance(state: scan_open_header(state: state, text: text))
    } else {
      scan_state_advance(
        state: scan_state_refuse(
          state: state,
          text: text,
          detail: "structural line is neither an item header the rust grammar authority names, nor a use declaration, attribute or comment"
        )
      )
    }
  }
}

fn scan_rust_source(repo_path: String, content: String) -> RustFileScan {
  let lines = split(s: content, delimiter: "\n")
  let final = fold_list(
    xs: lines,
    empty: RustScanState {
      repo_path: repo_path,
      line_number: 1,
      items: [],
      macro_regions: [],
      scopes: [],
      attach_start: 0,
      refusal_line: 0,
      refusal_text: "",
      refusal_detail: "",
      continues_string: false
    },
    cons: fn(acc, line) { scan_state_mark_continuation(state: scan_step(state: acc, text: line), text: line) }
  )
  if scan_state_refused(state: final) {
    RustFileScanRefused {
      repo_path: repo_path,
      line: final.refusal_line,
      line_text: final.refusal_text,
      detail: final.refusal_detail
    }
  } else if length(xs: final.scopes) > 0 {
    fold_list(
      xs: list_last_scope(xs: final.scopes),
      empty: RustFileScanRefused {
        repo_path: repo_path,
        line: 1,
        line_text: "",
        detail: "scope stack is non-empty but carries no scope"
      },
      cons: fn(_acc, scope) {
        RustFileScanRefused {
          repo_path: repo_path,
          line: scope_start_line(scope: scope),
          line_text: "",
          detail: "scope is still open at end of file -- its closing brace was never reached at its own indent"
        }
      }
    )
  } else {
    RustFileScanned {
      repo_path: repo_path,
      items: final.items,
      macro_regions: final.macro_regions,
      line_count: length(xs: lines)
    }
  }
}

fn scan_item_keys(items: List<RustItemIdentity>) -> List<String> {
  list_map(xs: items, f: fn(item) { rust_item_identity_key(item: item) })
}

fn item_contains_line(item: RustItemIdentity, line: Int) -> Bool {
  line >= item.start_line && line <= item.start_line + item.loc - 1
}

// ATTRIBUTION HAS NO UNKNOWN ARM, AND THAT IS A PROPERTY OF THE SCAN RATHER THAN OPTIMISM HERE.
// scan_rust_source refuses on every structural line it cannot classify, so on a RustFileScanned
// every line is either inside an item or is a recognized file-level line. The only residual
// question is whether the line exists at all, which is why the third arm is a range fact and not a
// "could not tell". A caller holding a RustFileScanRefused already has its refusal and never
// reaches this function.
//
// THE INNERMOST CONTAINING ITEM WINS. A method's lines are inside both the method and its impl
// block, and a module's members are inside the module; the containing item with the greatest start
// line is the one the change is about. Reporting the outer one would attribute a method edit to the
// impl block, which is precisely the mis-attribution that would let a new capability land inside an
// enclosing scope with no addition to report.
fn macro_region_contains_line(region: RustMacroRegion, line: Int) -> Bool {
  line >= region.start_line && line <= region.start_line + region.loc - 1
}

fn attribute_scanned_line(
  items: List<RustItemIdentity>,
  macro_regions: List<RustMacroRegion>,
  line_count: Int,
  line: Int
) -> RustLineAttribution {
  if line < 1 || line > line_count {
    AttributionOutOfRange { line: line, line_count: line_count }
  } else {
    let hits = filter(xs: items, predicate: fn(item) { item_contains_line(item: item, line: line) })
    let innermost = fold_list(
      xs: hits,
      empty: 0,
      cons: fn(best, item) { if item.start_line > best { item.start_line } else { best } }
    )
    fold_list(
      xs: filter(xs: hits, predicate: fn(item) { item.start_line == innermost }),
      empty: fold_list(
        xs: filter(
          xs: macro_regions,
          predicate: fn(region) { macro_region_contains_line(region: region, line: line) }
        ),
        empty: AttributedToFileLevel,
        cons: fn(_acc, region) {
          AttributedToUnnamedMacroBlock { macro_name: region.macro_name, line: line }
        }
      ),
      cons: fn(_acc, item) { AttributedToItem { item_key: rust_item_identity_key(item: item) } }
    )
  }
}
